In article <2b84b715%fidonet.org@p1.f44.n242.z2.fidonet.org> Benedikt_Heinen@p1.firemark.fido.de (Benedikt Heinen) writes:
>just a short question, what about adding a feature in Nethack (or writing an 
>external program for this) to merge several high score tables into one, so that 
>people at different locations can exchange their RECORDS files, merge these and 
>see how the other people rank compared to oneself...

I've done this with PC Hack 3.61, and 3.0.  What I did was to write a simple
little Pascal program.  Ok, it wasn't so simple, as I played with keeping track
of different statistics too, but the idea was very simple.  I'd imagine that it
would be even easier using unix awk or VMS sort/merge.  If you didn't mind a
bit of editing, you could always do it by hand, as the scores are all one line
of an ASCII file each.

It's fun when you've only got PC's, but you want to see the interesting
character names/modes of dying that your friends have on their lists.  It is
less interesting as everyone gets good, and the top n scores are always "so and
so the demigod ascended with 10e99 points"

